{
  "title": "Reflux: reverse flow and infection",
  "sources": [
    "CH37S22",
    "CH38S30",
    "CH39S39",
    "CH39S40"
  ],
  "stages": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "title": "Identify the junction",
      "text": "Vesicoureteral reflux reverses the normal direction of urinary flow. Begin at the bladder and identify where each ureter enters."
    },
    {
      "start": 8.84,
      "title": "Normal protection",
      "text": "The normal junction helps prevent urine from moving backward during bladder emptying."
    },
    {
      "start": 13.24,
      "title": "Retrograde flow",
      "text": "In reflux, a congenital junction abnormality or abnormal ureteral insertion can allow urine to travel back toward the ureter and kidney. Watch the direction change along the same anatomy."
    },
    {
      "start": 25.37,
      "title": "Ascending infection",
      "text": "When the bladder urine is infected, reflux can carry that infection upward and encourage repeated pyelonephritis."
    },
    {
      "start": 32.87,
      "title": "Findings and limits",
      "text": "The supplied chapter describes silent cases as well as recurrent urinary infections, unexplained fever, and poor growth. It lists prompt treatment of infection and intervention for recurrent or high-grade reflux. This visualization shows the direction and consequence of reflux. It does not invent the missing grade-by-grade criteria, and its flow speed and volume are schematic."
    }
  ],
